00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.730
So we'll start at the beginning
with cloud IAM concepts.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:48.870
IAM is about who can do
what on which resource.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.840
Here are the different who's.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.060
There are four types of
identities that you'll

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.950
be using in Google Cloud.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:57.960
The first is a Google account.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.700
This could be someone's
Gmail account.

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:03.060
It could be an account as
part of your G Suite domain.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.640
These are accounts that
belong to human beings.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:06.750
They can call APIs.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.970
They can log in
interactively to the console.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:13.482
The second type of identity
is a service account.

00:04:13.482 --> 00:04:15.440
And this is the identity
that your code will be

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.839
running as on Compute Engine.

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:21.230
So for example, if you're using
Compute Engine and BigQuery

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:24.200
to do data analysis, you
could grant your code,

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.357
or the instance it runs
on, a service account.

00:04:26.357 --> 00:04:28.940
And grant that service account
permission to the BigQuery data

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:31.067
that you need.

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:33.150
It's important to note
service accounts cannot log

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.220
in interactively to the console.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.850
We still have a ways before
the robots take over.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:42.520
The third identity
is a G Suite Domain.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.250
This represents everyone
in your G Suite Domain

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:48.260
and is a way of sharing
your resource very broadly.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.070
For example, if you wanted
to share a particular image

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:53.200
with everyone in
your organization,

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.935
you can grant a role
to the G Suite Domain.

00:04:55.935 --> 00:04:57.310
And the fourth,
and the one we'll

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:01.080
be using most commonly for IAM
policies, is a Google group.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:03.870
A group can have
any of these four--

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.495
a Google account,
service account,

00:05:05.495 --> 00:05:07.620
or a G Suite Domain, or
another group inside of it.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.710
So what happens now when
someone calls an API?

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.910
Let's say they're calling
the instances get method

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:17.320
on compute.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:19.070
Well, in some cases,
Compute Engine

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:22.630
will ask one-to-one with IAM.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:24.760
Compute instances
get is the method.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:29.800
Compute instance get permission
is what we check for with IAM.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.464
Other times, it's one-to-many.

00:05:32.464 --> 00:05:34.380
If you try to create an
instance, we'll check,

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.300
do you have permission
to create the instance?

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.799
Do you have permission to use
the network that you're trying

00:05:38.799 --> 00:05:40.390
to attach the instance to?

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:43.830
Do you have permission to use
the image that you specified?

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:44.960
So sometimes, one-to-one.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.870
Sometimes, one-to-many.

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:49.370
Here's the what.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:53.680
You can give these identities
permissions through IAM roles.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.394
Now, roles are just
sets of permissions.

00:05:56.394 --> 00:05:58.310
For example, if you
wanted to allow developers

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:01.037
in your organization to
create instances, and manage

00:06:01.037 --> 00:06:03.620
them, and delete them, you could
grant them the instance admin

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:04.120
role.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:07.850
It includes permissions to
start, stop, create, delete,

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.190
and manage instances.

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:14.210
You can grant these roles to
an identity, like a group,

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:15.545
on a resource such as a project.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:21.260
There are three
types of IAM roles.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:25.220
And the one to use depends
on your organization's needs.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:26.870
First, a little bit of history.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:28.460
When we started
with Compute Engine,

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:31.610
we started with three
broad primitive roles--

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:34.600
owner, editor, and viewer.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:38.420
Now, these gave broad
access across services.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:41.570
An editor could create an
instance on Compute Engine.

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:45.320
It could also create
a Cloud SQL instance.

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.210
Last year at Next,
we announced that we

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:52.630
had IAM roles to provide
more granular permission.

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:56.140
Today, we have over 70
predefined IAM roles.

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:57.940
You can use these
to grant an employee

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:01.396
access to specific
parts of Google Cloud.

00:07:01.396 --> 00:07:03.520
For example, if you wanted
to allow your networking

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.010
and security team to
manage all the networks,

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:08.560
you could grant them the
compute network admin role.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.810
That would not allow them
to spin up instances,

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:12.730
but they could do
network management,

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:14.710
create new networks,
subnets, and routes.

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:19.890
And the third, as we
announced yesterday,

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:23.610
is custom roles, which we
have available in alpha.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:26.829
This allows you to
use which permission

00:07:26.829 --> 00:07:27.870
you want to give someone.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:30.930
If you only want them to call
three particular methods,

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.820
you could create that role.

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:35.560
You could create
them from scratch--

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:37.212
not recommended.

00:07:37.212 --> 00:07:38.920
The best practice
would be to create them

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:40.370
from an existing role.

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:42.970
So if you have, for example,
an entitlement system

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:46.910
already on premises,
think about how

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:49.450
you want that
employee's access to map

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:52.100
to Google Cloud,
which methods you

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:53.780
want them to be able to call.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:56.450
Find the predefined role that
most closely matches that,

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:59.334
and then add or remove
to get your custom role.

00:07:59.334 --> 00:08:01.250
For example, if you
wanted to allow developers

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:04.520
to create instances, but
not modify the network

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:06.590
perimeter by assigning
an external IP address,

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:10.590
you could remove
that permission.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:15.610
And the final concept around IAM
is that of resource hierarchy.

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.730
IAM is about who can do
what on which resource.

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:19.410
Here are the resources.

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.210
At the top, it's your
organization node,

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:23.480
which is tied to your domain.

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:26.620
And below that you can create
a folder for each division

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:28.502
or for each team.

00:08:28.502 --> 00:08:29.960
And within those
folders, you could

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:33.350
create folders for
product or a project

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:35.602
for a particular service.

00:08:35.602 --> 00:08:38.870
And then within that, specific
resources, like instances,

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:42.780
or subnets, or service accounts.

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:45.570
The project provides
the isolation

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:48.360
between different teams
and different services

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:49.770
in your organization.

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:53.910
So think of putting each
individual micro-service down

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:55.620
at the bottom of this
resource hierarchy.

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:57.536
We'll talk about that
more a little bit later.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:08:59.940
OK.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:04.830
So there's the summary
of IAM concept.

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:06.460
Now, we're off and running.

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:08.382
This is [? Louvre ?] again.

00:09:08.382 --> 00:09:10.215
He's a little bit older,
a little bit wiser.

00:09:10.215 --> 00:09:13.980
He knows a little bit about
Cloud IAM at this point.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:17.310
Now we'll go into Compute
Engine, and a few specific ways

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:19.825
that it works, and how it
affects your IAM strategy.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:25.450
First, if you're
thinking about security,

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:27.730
you're thinking about, what
are the bad outcomes that

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:29.090
are possible?

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:31.090
Not necessarily
fun to think about,

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:32.526
but important to think about.

00:09:32.526 --> 00:09:34.400
And whenever you have
a machine, whether it's

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:37.360
a machine on premises,
a virtual machine,

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:41.250
or in the cloud, what would
happen if someone unauthorized

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.690
gained access to it?

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:45.700
So you want to provide
mechanisms such

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:48.850
that you can limit what
an instance can access

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:51.340
as a way of reducing risk.

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:56.930
Now, the first such mechanism
are OAuth access scopes.

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:59.800
This was in the
pre-IAM world, where

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:02.830
we had these three primitive
roles-- owner, editor,

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:04.650
and viewer.

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:07.470
And each project had
one service account

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:11.320
that all instances ran as.

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:13.390
Well, if you have
this one account that

00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:15.887
has rather broad privilege,
you want some way

00:10:15.887 --> 00:10:18.220
to be able to limit your
particular instance or instance

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:20.310
group to just what you need.

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:23.120
And access scopes
were a way to do that.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:25.790
They're relatively broad
in their granularity.

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:27.706
For example, you could
give someone permission

00:10:27.706 --> 00:10:29.970
to just the compute API
or the read-only methods

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:32.784
in the compute API.

00:10:32.784 --> 00:10:35.520
Now, there was one special
scope, the Cloud platform

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:40.200
scope, which allowed access
to all of Google Cloud APIs.

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:44.260
We'll talk about that one
more a little bit later.

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:47.670
Now with IAM, you
could use IAM roles

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:50.640
as the mechanism to limit
what your instance can

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:52.950
access through an API.

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:54.490
We have over 70 such roles.

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:59.010
It offers a lot more flexibility
and granularity than scopes.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.650
And instances today can each run
as their own service account.

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:06.000
So each component of your app
could have its own identity

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:08.760
and its own level of access to
the resources in your project

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:11.172
or in your organization.

00:11:11.172 --> 00:11:12.630
And these roles,
as we saw earlier,

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:15.270
can be much more fine-grained
than these broader

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:16.170
access scopes.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:22.780
One of the common
operations when

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:24.279
someone needs to
create an instance,

00:11:24.279 --> 00:11:26.590
you want to go in and
see, what do you have?

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:29.650
Maybe you're going in to set
something up for the first time

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:32.350
or to troubleshoot an issue.

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:34.780
We wanted to make that
easy in Compute Engine.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:39.070
So if you go to our web console
and you click the SSH button,

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:41.530
magic happens and in you are.

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:42.730
So what do we actually do?

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.610
And what's the impact on IAM?

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:47.380
When you click on
this SSH button,

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:50.240
we generate a new key pair.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:52.430
And we'll send the
public key into the guest

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:54.170
using our metadata service.

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:57.382
That's a key value
pair data store.

00:11:57.382 --> 00:11:59.090
Then we use the
corresponding private key

00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:00.006
to start your session.

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:04.170
The reason why I
bring this up is

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:06.420
that identity and
access management is not

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:08.400
just about IAM roles.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:11.280
Let's think about, what are all
of the identities, including

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:15.720
SSH keys, and what can
they access in the guest?

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:17.970
So there are two options there.

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:21.270
First is automatic
key management.

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:23.220
If people in your
organization will only

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:27.510
need SSH using either
the Google Web Console

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:29.360
or using the gcloud
command line tool,

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:30.610
you don't have to do anything.

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:32.370
We'll create the key pairs.

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:36.270
If necessary, we'll create a
new one if they've expired.

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:39.290
SSH just works.

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:42.530
On the other hand, a lot
of you have existing SSH

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:45.360
key management on premises.

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:49.100
So if you want to continue to
manually manage those SSH keys,

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:53.090
you could do that by setting
them on the SSH keys metadata

00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:55.940
field, and that would
put the public keys

00:12:55.940 --> 00:12:58.669
into your instances.

00:12:58.669 --> 00:13:00.460
If you want to allow
all of your developers

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:03.726
to SSH into all the
VMs in your project,

00:13:03.726 --> 00:13:06.100
you could set the public key
once on the project metadata

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:08.812
and it gets inherited
by all instances.

00:13:08.812 --> 00:13:10.520
On the other hand, if
you wanted to allow

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:14.060
a contractor or a vendor
access to a specific instance

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:16.790
to troubleshoot an issue, you
could put their public key

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:18.034
on the instance metadata.

00:13:18.034 --> 00:13:18.950
So there's two levels.

00:13:18.950 --> 00:13:22.920
There's a resource
hierarchy there as well.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:26.010
I should note that SSH is
a particularly powerful

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:28.040
permission to give someone.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:31.680
First, anyone who
can SSH in by default

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:34.220
is also part of the
[INAUDIBLE] group.

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:36.600
But secondly, they
can also run commands

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:39.750
as that instance's
service account.

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:41.990
So there's a way for
someone to get access

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:44.490
to resources that you may
not have give them directly.

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:46.198
And I'll show you what
we did about that.

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:52.360
We treat a service account
both as an identity

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:54.532
and as a resource.

00:13:54.532 --> 00:13:56.740
It's an identity in the
sense that if your code needs

00:13:56.740 --> 00:13:59.770
to call BigQuery, you
could grant an IAM

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:03.470
role to your instance's service
account to access BigQuery.

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:08.620
It's a resource in
the sense that you

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:11.380
can grant users or
groups permission

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:13.615
to act as that service account.

00:14:13.615 --> 00:14:15.490
And we wanted this to
be an explicit decision

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:16.073
that you make.

00:14:16.073 --> 00:14:19.725
We don't want there to be
any elevation of privilege.

00:14:19.725 --> 00:14:21.955
We want it to be
an explicit choice.

00:14:21.955 --> 00:14:24.560
So here's what this
could look like.

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:28.660
Suppose we had a project where
a user is an instance admin.

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:30.682
They can create and
delete instances.

00:14:30.682 --> 00:14:32.890
This is the sign for creating
and deleting instances,

00:14:32.890 --> 00:14:34.870
by the way.

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:39.040
And then, you give the service
account access to BigQuery

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:43.900
so it can run queries, create
saved queries, write new data.

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:46.810
If we didn't treat the
service account as a resource,

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:48.419
here's what could happen.

00:14:48.419 --> 00:14:49.960
You could have the
user trying to get

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:52.300
a data set from BigQuery.

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:54.480
BigQuery would check
with Cloud IAM.

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:56.200
IAM would check
against the IAM policy.

00:14:56.200 --> 00:15:00.120
It would actually check against
all policies in the hierarchy.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:03.300
IAM would rightly say no and
BigQuery would rightly say no.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:06.540
So far, so good.

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:08.400
Now, suppose that
this instance admin

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:12.030
creates an instance and
Compute Engine checks with IAM.

00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:14.355
Oh, you have permission
to create instances.

00:15:14.355 --> 00:15:16.590
You have permission
to create disks.

00:15:16.590 --> 00:15:21.670
Here's your instance running
as a service account.

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:24.180
And now that user could SSH in
and use the service account's

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:27.720
credential to access that data
in BigQuery that we didn't

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:30.030
want them to have access to.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:32.860
That's not what we want.

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:34.840
Instead, we want it to
be an explicit choice.

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:37.750
So if someone does
not have permission

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:41.360
to act as that service account
which you've given them,

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:42.650
then this would happen.

00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:46.062
BigQuery, again, will
say no, as it should.

00:15:46.062 --> 00:15:48.520
But when they ask Compute Engine
to create an instance that

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:50.561
runs at that service
account, we will also check,

00:15:50.561 --> 00:15:53.470
do you have permission to
act as the service account?

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:55.500
IAM will say no.

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:57.690
We'll say no.

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:01.060
No elevation of privilege.

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:03.970
We wanted to avoid surprises.

00:16:03.970 --> 00:16:07.240
If you want someone to
act as another identity,

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:08.920
it's an explicit
choice that you make.

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:16.000
Service account actor role
is the predefined role

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:18.960
that grants this
"act as" permission.

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:22.780
And Compute Engine checks for
this permission in two cases.

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:26.020
First is if the API would
cause different code

00:16:26.020 --> 00:16:28.129
to run inside the guest.

00:16:28.129 --> 00:16:30.670
For example, if you're setting
a startup script or a shutdown

00:16:30.670 --> 00:16:32.116
script.

00:16:32.116 --> 00:16:33.490
We will check you
have permission

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:35.890
not only to set that metadata,
but you have permission

00:16:35.890 --> 00:16:37.924
to act as that service account.

00:16:37.924 --> 00:16:40.090
And similarly, if you're
going to change the service

00:16:40.090 --> 00:16:41.950
account that the
instance runs as,

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:44.560
do you have permission to act
as that new service account?

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:48.060
All right.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:49.540
That's the background.

00:16:49.540 --> 00:16:51.390
We've covered the IAM
concepts, a little bit

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:52.750
about Compute Engine.

00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:56.680
Now we're onto identity
best practices.

00:16:56.680 --> 00:17:01.540
Identity here embodied by Milo,
who is a chihuahua-rat terrier

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:05.650
mix, or a spider at Google scale
depending on who you believe.

00:17:08.619 --> 00:17:13.230
The first best practice is to
reuse your existing identities.

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:15.000
How many of you have
an on-premises LDAP

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:17.150
server right now?

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:17.650
OK.

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:20.540
That's about 3/4 of you.

00:17:20.540 --> 00:17:24.822
If you have an on-premises LDAP
server, like Active Directory,

00:17:24.822 --> 00:17:26.780
you can install the Google
Cloud Directory Sync

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:32.020
tool, a free download, to
sync one way users and groups

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:34.880
from your LDAP
server into G Suite.

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:37.380
So you can continue managing
users and groups the way

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:40.940
you always have been and now be
able to grant them permission

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:41.930
to cloud resources.

00:17:44.624 --> 00:17:46.290
The second best
practice around identity

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:51.050
is to not store keys or
secrets in your code.

00:17:51.050 --> 00:17:52.490
You've probably
heard news stories

00:17:52.490 --> 00:17:56.450
about people scanning
GitHub looking for API keys,

00:17:56.450 --> 00:18:00.740
or heard about the Truffle Hog
project to help you find them.

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:02.360
We wanted to make
it easy to make sure

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:05.990
that you don't have to
manage keys or access

00:18:05.990 --> 00:18:09.940
tokens in most cases.

00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:11.760
If your code is running
on Compute Engine

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:13.600
and you need to call
another Google API,

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:18.460
like BigQuery, just install the
client library for the language

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:19.240
that you need.

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:22.787
Python client library, for
example, and things just work.

00:18:22.787 --> 00:18:24.370
You don't have to
get an access token.

00:18:24.370 --> 00:18:26.869
You don't have to create a key
pair for the service account.

00:18:26.869 --> 00:18:29.434
You just call the
API and it works.

00:18:29.434 --> 00:18:30.850
If you're doing
local development,

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:34.090
you can download the same
client library to your laptop.

00:18:34.090 --> 00:18:38.136
You can create a new key pair
for that service account.

00:18:38.136 --> 00:18:42.990
And then set the Google
application credentials

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:46.000
environment variable to
the path to that key.

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:48.840
You don't have to put
it into your code,

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:52.350
and then the calls just work.

00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:54.150
We want it to be
easy that you can not

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:56.730
have to manage tokens and keys.

00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:02.100
The next identity
best practice--

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:06.170
because again, identity is more
than just about IAM roles--

00:19:06.170 --> 00:19:09.634
is requiring more
secure authentication.

00:19:09.634 --> 00:19:11.550
You have heard the
identity as a service talk.

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:13.537
And in there, a lot
of folks mentioned

00:19:13.537 --> 00:19:15.620
how they were already using
two-factor auth, which

00:19:15.620 --> 00:19:17.400
is awesome.

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:21.230
And we also announced security
keys, a more phishing-resistant

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:25.820
token that you can use to
identify a particular user.

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:28.040
And you can enable this
on your G Suite Domain

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:30.150
just by going into
the Admin Console

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:31.400
and clicking the radio button.

00:19:33.531 --> 00:19:35.530
The second set of identities
to think about then

00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:40.360
are people who can SSH
in to your instances.

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:43.780
First, we recommend disabling
password authentication

00:19:43.780 --> 00:19:47.270
because key pairs are
a more secure way.

00:19:47.270 --> 00:19:49.740
And secondly,
disable root login.

00:19:49.740 --> 00:19:52.057
So if someone has to
run an elevated command,

00:19:52.057 --> 00:19:54.390
you have a better chance at
finding that in the auth log

00:19:54.390 --> 00:19:56.940
afterwards.

00:19:56.940 --> 00:19:59.576
Compute Engine images
already do these.

00:19:59.576 --> 00:20:01.200
There are a lot of
other best practices

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:04.420
around image management that
I couldn't do justice to here.

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:06.783
So there's a doc for
more information.

00:20:10.860 --> 00:20:14.920
That brings us to
least privilege.

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:17.000
[? Dico ?] is a
bullmastiff and boxer

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:19.097
who embodies least privilege.

00:20:19.097 --> 00:20:20.180
Good luck getting through.

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:22.513
There's two chairs and there's
[? Dico ?] in the middle.

00:20:26.990 --> 00:20:29.520
So the first best practice
around this on Compute Engine

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:32.680
is obviously granting
predefined or custom roles--

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:35.360
usually.

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:38.262
Depending on how you've set
up your resource hierarchy,

00:20:38.262 --> 00:20:40.220
there may be times where
you could give someone

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:44.000
like your networking team the
owner role on a project that

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:46.050
only has networking resources.

00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:49.674
We'll talk about that
more in a few minutes.

00:20:49.674 --> 00:20:51.090
There are some
operations that are

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:53.890
particularly powerful that
you want to be aware of.

00:20:53.890 --> 00:20:56.740
The first is setting
an IAM policy.

00:20:56.740 --> 00:20:58.180
Someone who can
set an IAM policy

00:20:58.180 --> 00:20:59.800
could grant
themselves or someone

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:03.890
else any role on that resource.

00:21:03.890 --> 00:21:06.630
So by default, the owner
and the organization admins

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:10.709
are the two roles that
have that permission.

00:21:10.709 --> 00:21:13.250
The second powerful operation
is acting as a service account,

00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:15.280
as we talked about
a bit earlier.

00:21:15.280 --> 00:21:17.120
Because if you can
access a service account,

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:20.000
you can now get access to
data that that service account

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:22.162
has access to.

00:21:22.162 --> 00:21:23.620
You can do things
like set metadata

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:26.695
to modify startup
and shutdown scripts.

00:21:26.695 --> 00:21:29.070
And there are three roles that
have that-- owner, editor,

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:31.910
and service account actor.

00:21:31.910 --> 00:21:33.764
The best practice
on compute is then

00:21:33.764 --> 00:21:35.680
that you give the service
account active role,

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:38.140
that permission to act
as a service account,

00:21:38.140 --> 00:21:40.630
you grant that on a
particular service account

00:21:40.630 --> 00:21:42.490
rather than on the project.

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:44.560
If you grant it on
the project, the group

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:47.500
that you gave access to can
now access all of the service

00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:49.180
accounts in your
project, including

00:21:49.180 --> 00:21:51.550
those they may have
nothing to do with.

00:21:51.550 --> 00:21:54.070
There's one exception.

00:21:54.070 --> 00:21:58.380
If you want someone to be able
to set project-level metadata,

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:00.630
if you want someone to be
able to set a common startup

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:04.460
script for all instances
in your project,

00:22:04.460 --> 00:22:07.280
give them the service account
actor role on the project.

00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:10.616
And the reason for that is that
if you set project metadata,

00:22:10.616 --> 00:22:12.740
we don't want to just check,
do you have permission

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:14.864
to all the existing instances.

00:22:14.864 --> 00:22:16.280
Although, that
startup script will

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:18.055
affect all existing instances.

00:22:18.055 --> 00:22:19.430
They'll also affect
all instances

00:22:19.430 --> 00:22:21.030
you create in the future.

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:22.310
And that's why we want to
check for that permission

00:22:22.310 --> 00:22:23.720
at the project
level and not just

00:22:23.720 --> 00:22:24.950
on each individual instance.

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:31.830
Anytime we talk about
best practices around IAM,

00:22:31.830 --> 00:22:36.690
we're sort of duty bound to
mention use groups, not users.

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:38.430
I won't belabor this point.

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:42.180
I will say one thing if you're
managing groups in G Suite,

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:46.520
not on your on-premises server,
then that's in the Admin

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:49.040
Console, admin.google.com.

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:51.170
Separate from the
IAM policies you'll

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:53.282
be setting in the Cloud console.

00:22:53.282 --> 00:22:54.990
That's all I'm going
to say about groups.

00:22:58.060 --> 00:23:00.940
So I've talked a little bit
about least privilege to users.

00:23:00.940 --> 00:23:04.210
Also, least privilege is
necessary for applications.

00:23:04.210 --> 00:23:07.780
Suppose that you have a
content streaming company.

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:09.610
And you want to
both stream content

00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:11.950
and also do some data analytics.

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:14.410
Maybe understand which
next piece of content

00:23:14.410 --> 00:23:19.754
to recommend, which ad to serve
up, how to increase conversion.

00:23:19.754 --> 00:23:21.170
The best practice
here is that you

00:23:21.170 --> 00:23:23.750
give each instance
or group of instances

00:23:23.750 --> 00:23:24.950
their own service account.

00:23:24.950 --> 00:23:28.190
In this case, the content
streaming instances

00:23:28.190 --> 00:23:31.422
have one identity, the content
stream or service account.

00:23:31.422 --> 00:23:33.380
And the instance you're
using for data analysis

00:23:33.380 --> 00:23:36.374
have the data analyzer
service account.

00:23:36.374 --> 00:23:38.290
You could grant each of
those service accounts

00:23:38.290 --> 00:23:40.979
just the IAM roles
on just the resources

00:23:40.979 --> 00:23:42.520
that they each need
to do their jobs.

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:48.370
But then, give each
of these instances

00:23:48.370 --> 00:23:50.740
a very permissive scope.

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:55.140
Remember, we talked about
scopes and IAM roles earlier.

00:23:55.140 --> 00:23:57.290
It seems a little
odd that we would

00:23:57.290 --> 00:24:01.160
say in a least privilege
section of a session grant

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:03.590
very permissive scopes.

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:05.240
Here's why that's
going to be OK.

00:24:07.980 --> 00:24:11.600
When your instance is
making an API request,

00:24:11.600 --> 00:24:13.740
there are two mechanisms
of authorization

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:16.050
that it has to pass.

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:18.910
So when this VM is
querying BigQuery,

00:24:18.910 --> 00:24:20.910
we automatically-- because
you're running inside

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:22.368
of Compute Engine,
we automatically

00:24:22.368 --> 00:24:23.860
get the access token.

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:26.370
That access token
includes both the identity

00:24:26.370 --> 00:24:32.310
of the service account as well
as a set of access scopes.

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:35.380
What BigQuery will
then do is first check,

00:24:35.380 --> 00:24:38.290
is this set of
scopes something that

00:24:38.290 --> 00:24:41.060
allows this API to be called?

00:24:41.060 --> 00:24:44.060
And if so, then go
and ask Cloud IAM,

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:47.420
do you have-- does this
service account have access

00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:50.340
to this resource?

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:53.430
Because you have Cloud IAM as
the definitive authorization

00:24:53.430 --> 00:24:56.100
store for Google
Cloud platform, you

00:24:56.100 --> 00:24:59.010
could leave the
scopes wide open.

00:24:59.010 --> 00:25:02.990
That makes it easier to
configure your instances,

00:25:02.990 --> 00:25:06.510
but doesn't leave you in
any more of a risky state.

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:09.030
Because even if someone
has wide open scopes,

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:10.522
IAM is still the
definitive answer

00:25:10.522 --> 00:25:12.230
as to whether they
can access a resource.

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:19.400
I'd also be remiss if I
didn't mention key rotation.

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:21.770
And there are two sets
of keys that you'd

00:25:21.770 --> 00:25:23.400
be interested in here.

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:26.320
One is keys for the
service account.

00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:29.650
For example, if you're
querying a service

00:25:29.650 --> 00:25:32.930
outside of Google Cloud, you
need to export your own service

00:25:32.930 --> 00:25:33.940
keys.

00:25:33.940 --> 00:25:36.070
You can do key rotation
by creating a new key

00:25:36.070 --> 00:25:39.190
with the create API, replace
the old one with a new one,

00:25:39.190 --> 00:25:41.700
and then delete the old one.

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:45.080
Public keys for SSH are
treated a little differently

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:47.260
in that instance
metadata is written

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:49.880
in a read/modify/write paradigm.

00:25:49.880 --> 00:25:52.400
So add the new key,
set the metadata,

00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:54.677
do the key replacement,
and then remove the key

00:25:54.677 --> 00:25:55.760
by setting metadata again.

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:05.460
For similar reasons
that you don't

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:08.820
want to store secrets
or keys in code,

00:26:08.820 --> 00:26:12.282
so, too, in instance metadata.

00:26:12.282 --> 00:26:13.740
The purpose of the
metadata service

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:17.530
is to provide configuration
information between the Google

00:26:17.530 --> 00:26:20.330
cloud APIs and your instances.

00:26:20.330 --> 00:26:23.590
So things like startup scripts,
shutdown scripts, public SSH

00:26:23.590 --> 00:26:26.009
keys.

00:26:26.009 --> 00:26:28.550
If this metadata is treated as
part of the instance resource.

00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:30.860
So anyone who can
get an instance

00:26:30.860 --> 00:26:34.590
will be able to see all of
the metadata on that instance.

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:39.480
So if, for example, your
instances use a private token

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:43.390
to access a service that
you're managing yourself,

00:26:43.390 --> 00:26:45.150
store that token
or that password

00:26:45.150 --> 00:26:47.620
or that key in Cloud
Storage and then

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:49.960
give access to the
instance's service

00:26:49.960 --> 00:26:52.110
account to read the information.

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:54.430
That way, anyone who lists
the instance won't see it,

00:26:54.430 --> 00:26:56.388
but your instance will
still have access to it.

00:27:01.580 --> 00:27:05.840
That brings us to the third
section, centralized control.

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:10.310
Embodied here by Kira, a
Klee Kai who has centralized

00:27:10.310 --> 00:27:12.770
control of this couch and
also part of the fireplace.

00:27:16.787 --> 00:27:18.870
The first best practice
around centralized control

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:21.689
is to bring your organization's
existing structure

00:27:21.689 --> 00:27:22.230
to the cloud.

00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:28.320
If you have, for example,
a lot of developers

00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:31.374
who are allowed to create
cloud projects, what

00:27:31.374 --> 00:27:32.790
happens if one of
those developers

00:27:32.790 --> 00:27:37.430
leaves the company and those
resources are still going?

00:27:37.430 --> 00:27:41.240
With the organization node,
anyone who creates a project,

00:27:41.240 --> 00:27:44.900
if their email address
is part of your domain,

00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:47.600
that's automatically part
of the organization node.

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:50.240
And therefore, your
organization admins

00:27:50.240 --> 00:27:54.140
can set policies to allow a
particular group of individuals

00:27:54.140 --> 00:27:56.570
to gain access to them
in case, for example,

00:27:56.570 --> 00:27:58.657
the project owner leaves.

00:27:58.657 --> 00:28:00.740
You could also then take
advantage of organization

00:28:00.740 --> 00:28:03.080
policies that my
colleagues Ray and Ray

00:28:03.080 --> 00:28:06.890
discussed yesterday in IO 211
to set the ground rules for what

00:28:06.890 --> 00:28:09.140
people are allowed to do
within the organization,

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:11.840
even if they have relative
autonomy within their project.

00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:17.410
The best practices
here are first,

00:28:17.410 --> 00:28:20.230
use the org node for
centralized control.

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:24.520
Map your company's domain
to your organization node,

00:28:24.520 --> 00:28:28.150
and then create folders
for divisions or teams.

00:28:28.150 --> 00:28:31.720
Projects for each service,
for each environment.

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:35.710
So you have one project for
a team's dev environment,

00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:37.870
test environment,
and prod environment.

00:28:37.870 --> 00:28:40.505
That way, you can
give stricter control

00:28:40.505 --> 00:28:42.880
over that prod environment
while allowing developers more

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:44.470
freedom in dev and in test.

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:55.914
A lot of organizations have
standardized IT blessed images

00:28:55.914 --> 00:28:57.330
and want to make
it easier for you

00:28:57.330 --> 00:29:00.090
to share those images with
people in your organization

00:29:00.090 --> 00:29:02.490
so it's easy for them
to create the instance

00:29:02.490 --> 00:29:04.950
that you want them to.

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:06.530
The best practices
here are first,

00:29:06.530 --> 00:29:09.860
start with Google-provided
images as your base.

00:29:09.860 --> 00:29:12.200
We make optimizations
in these images

00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:14.750
to run them on Compute
Engine, and include

00:29:14.750 --> 00:29:16.280
already a lot of
the best practices

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:20.870
around image management, some
of which I alluded to earlier.

00:29:20.870 --> 00:29:24.080
The second best practice is to
publish your approved images

00:29:24.080 --> 00:29:26.120
to a shared project
and make those

00:29:26.120 --> 00:29:28.820
available to the
developers that need it.

00:29:28.820 --> 00:29:31.960
I'll show you an
example in a moment.

00:29:31.960 --> 00:29:34.520
And the third best
practice to be mindful of

00:29:34.520 --> 00:29:36.940
is when you're testing
these images before they

00:29:36.940 --> 00:29:40.550
have been approved, do that
testing in a separate project,

00:29:40.550 --> 00:29:42.680
not in the shared
images project.

00:29:42.680 --> 00:29:45.800
You don't want someone getting
a sneak peek at the next round

00:29:45.800 --> 00:29:48.450
of images before it's ready.

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:50.370
So here's what this
could look like.

00:29:50.370 --> 00:29:53.180
Suppose you want to give your
data scientists permission

00:29:53.180 --> 00:29:55.460
to create, update,
and delete instances,

00:29:55.460 --> 00:29:58.370
but you want them to
use your blessed images,

00:29:58.370 --> 00:30:00.620
the ones created
by your IT staff.

00:30:00.620 --> 00:30:03.800
So you could first create
a data analysis project,

00:30:03.800 --> 00:30:07.604
give the data scientist
group the instance admin role

00:30:07.604 --> 00:30:08.270
in that project.

00:30:08.270 --> 00:30:11.240
Now they can create
and delete instances.

00:30:11.240 --> 00:30:14.000
And then, have a separate
shared images project

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:16.620
where your IT staff
has the instance admin

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:19.310
role, so they can create
instances, delete them, create

00:30:19.310 --> 00:30:21.205
images and delete them.

00:30:21.205 --> 00:30:22.580
And then give the
image user role

00:30:22.580 --> 00:30:24.680
to the data scientist group.

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:27.700
So now as your IT staff
publishes approved images,

00:30:27.700 --> 00:30:29.689
the data scientists
just get access to them.

00:30:29.689 --> 00:30:31.730
They don't need to create
their own copy of them.

00:30:31.730 --> 00:30:34.063
They don't have to download
them into their own project.

00:30:34.063 --> 00:30:36.290
Now suddenly, you have
this proliferation.

00:30:36.290 --> 00:30:38.914
They can just use them directly
from the shared images project.

00:30:42.100 --> 00:30:48.000
Similarly for
controlling networks,

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:50.760
a lot of organizations
have a security team

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:53.790
that wants to have control
over all network traffic.

00:30:53.790 --> 00:30:56.610
You want to make sure that
you're routing traffic

00:30:56.610 --> 00:31:00.000
through a particular set of
firewall rules, or proxies.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:03.760
So we make that
easy in two ways.

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:06.630
One, if you're already a Compute
Engine customer before we

00:31:06.630 --> 00:31:09.780
announced cross-project
networking during the VPC

00:31:09.780 --> 00:31:14.340
session here, you may have a
number of different projects

00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:17.247
that already have networks
or subnets in them.

00:31:17.247 --> 00:31:19.080
You could give developers
the instance admin

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:21.121
role on that project, so
they can't monkey around

00:31:21.121 --> 00:31:23.086
with the firewalls,
the networking,

00:31:23.086 --> 00:31:25.460
and give your network and
security team the network admin

00:31:25.460 --> 00:31:27.251
and security admin
roles on those projects.

00:31:29.650 --> 00:31:32.950
If you're starting
something new,

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:34.780
you have an even
more flexible option

00:31:34.780 --> 00:31:37.996
available with
cross-project networking.

00:31:37.996 --> 00:31:40.930
And the recommendation there
is that you create one XPN

00:31:40.930 --> 00:31:44.104
host per network
that you're sharing,

00:31:44.104 --> 00:31:45.520
and then grant the
networking team

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:46.829
the owner role on that project.

00:31:46.829 --> 00:31:48.370
This is one of those
exceptions where

00:31:48.370 --> 00:31:51.790
you don't have to grant
them a very narrow role

00:31:51.790 --> 00:31:55.854
because that project is only
for networking resources.

00:31:55.854 --> 00:31:57.520
And the networking
team can grant access

00:31:57.520 --> 00:32:00.742
to other groups on
specific subnets.

00:32:00.742 --> 00:32:03.360
So this means that
the data scientists

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:08.580
can have easy communication
between all of their instances

00:32:08.580 --> 00:32:10.740
while still
maintaining isolation

00:32:10.740 --> 00:32:12.967
from other services
and other teams.

00:32:12.967 --> 00:32:14.550
So your policies
might look like this.

00:32:17.530 --> 00:32:19.800
In this case, we granted
the network user role

00:32:19.800 --> 00:32:21.800
to the data scientist
just on a specific subnet.

00:32:26.572 --> 00:32:28.030
And the last best
practice, this is

00:32:28.030 --> 00:32:30.280
like the opposite of camping.

00:32:30.280 --> 00:32:33.520
You very much want
to leave a trace.

00:32:33.520 --> 00:32:37.065
And even during this conference,
we got a reminder as to why.

00:32:37.065 --> 00:32:39.190
When Columbia Sportswear,
according to "The Seattle

00:32:39.190 --> 00:32:43.510
Times" sued a former
employee, who allegedly,

00:32:43.510 --> 00:32:47.430
on their last day of work, had
created a new user account,

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:50.487
granted broad access
to that user account,

00:32:50.487 --> 00:32:52.320
and then used it after
they left the company

00:32:52.320 --> 00:32:54.136
to access internal information.

00:32:57.400 --> 00:32:59.590
The best practices in
this case on compute--

00:32:59.590 --> 00:33:02.590
and some of these apply to
Google Cloud more broadly--

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:05.650
first is retain audit
logs in accordance

00:33:05.650 --> 00:33:10.612
with your business's risks
and mitigation strategies.

00:33:10.612 --> 00:33:12.820
The length of time that you
would maintain these logs

00:33:12.820 --> 00:33:15.790
depends on what risks you're
trying to protect against.

00:33:15.790 --> 00:33:18.490
And also, how many resources
you have to go and look

00:33:18.490 --> 00:33:22.070
into all this data afterwards.

00:33:22.070 --> 00:33:24.090
In Google Cloud,
you can set up logs

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:28.470
to be exported to Cloud Storage
or to BigQuery for easier data

00:33:28.470 --> 00:33:29.860
analysis.

00:33:29.860 --> 00:33:33.180
You can also have been them
set up to be sent via Pub/Subs.

00:33:33.180 --> 00:33:35.580
You can respond
in near real time

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:38.176
if something suspicious happens,
like someone setting an IAM

00:33:38.176 --> 00:33:39.300
policy that you don't like.

00:33:43.314 --> 00:33:44.730
There are other
organizations that

00:33:44.730 --> 00:33:48.690
need to also manage and monitor
what happens inside the guest.

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:50.850
For example, if
you need to monder

00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:52.890
the SSHD logs to
see who's trying

00:33:52.890 --> 00:33:55.540
to access your instances,
you could set up

00:33:55.540 --> 00:33:58.500
the Stackdriver logging agent
to forward those events from all

00:33:58.500 --> 00:34:00.530
of your instances
to a central place,

00:34:00.530 --> 00:34:02.484
and then you can look
for trends in who's

00:34:02.484 --> 00:34:04.150
trying to get into
your instances there.

00:34:09.429 --> 00:34:10.659
All right.

00:34:10.659 --> 00:34:11.949
Now is the demo section.

00:34:11.949 --> 00:34:15.820
Before we switch over, the
demo, unlike most sections,

00:34:15.820 --> 00:34:19.810
is embodied by two of
our team's dogs, not one.

00:34:19.810 --> 00:34:24.080
First is [? Kopi. ?]
[? Kopi's ?] a poodle,

00:34:24.080 --> 00:34:24.730
very playful.

00:34:24.730 --> 00:34:27.290
We're going to go play
around now in Compute Engine.

00:34:27.290 --> 00:34:31.270
That is a relatively
optimistic view of the demo.

00:34:31.270 --> 00:34:33.550
And Noodles embodies
another view,

00:34:33.550 --> 00:34:36.860
depending on how the demo goes.

00:34:36.860 --> 00:34:40.150
Also a chihuahua-terrier mix.

00:34:40.150 --> 00:34:40.650
All right.

00:34:40.650 --> 00:34:42.274
Can we switch over
to the demo, please?

00:34:45.409 --> 00:34:47.120
OK.

00:34:47.120 --> 00:34:52.330
So what we're looking at here is
an organization that I created.

00:34:52.330 --> 00:34:54.989
And we have a few
different departments.

00:34:54.989 --> 00:34:56.840
We have data
science, engineering,

00:34:56.840 --> 00:34:58.190
and shared resources.

00:34:58.190 --> 00:35:03.000
These are all folders in
the resource hierarchy.

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:07.100
And we have here the
data science dev project.

00:35:07.100 --> 00:35:10.869
We can examine the
IAM policy there.

00:35:10.869 --> 00:35:13.160
What you'll see is that we
granted the compute instance

00:35:13.160 --> 00:35:15.579
admin role to the
data scientist group.

00:35:15.579 --> 00:35:17.870
So they'll be able to create
and manage instances here.

00:35:21.105 --> 00:35:22.730
We also want those
same data scientists

00:35:22.730 --> 00:35:25.500
to have access to
the shared images.

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:29.359
So if we look at
the images project,

00:35:29.359 --> 00:35:31.400
you'll see that we granted
the compute image user

00:35:31.400 --> 00:35:35.000
role to that same group.

00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:36.650
And additionally,
because we want

00:35:36.650 --> 00:35:40.450
to maintain more control over
our networking and our firewall

00:35:40.450 --> 00:35:47.000
rules, we give them access to
a shared network in the XPN dev

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:47.540
project.

00:35:47.540 --> 00:35:50.480
And here they are,
that same group.

00:35:53.630 --> 00:35:55.610
Finally, because
they're data scientists,

00:35:55.610 --> 00:35:58.040
and data is one of their
favorite four-letter words,

00:35:58.040 --> 00:36:00.890
we're also granting them
the BigQuery data viewer

00:36:00.890 --> 00:36:06.350
role and the BigQuery user
role on our org data project.

00:36:06.350 --> 00:36:08.240
So they can access
all of this data.

00:36:12.780 --> 00:36:14.600
I'll show you one
other resource.

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:17.070
Since we talked about
controlling permissions

00:36:17.070 --> 00:36:21.060
to APIs that your
instances are calling,

00:36:21.060 --> 00:36:23.010
let's look at the data
science dev project.

00:36:27.099 --> 00:36:28.890
And we'll check the
service accounts there.

00:36:28.890 --> 00:36:30.181
There are two service accounts.

00:36:30.181 --> 00:36:32.340
One that Compute Engine
creates by default,

00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:35.700
and then I created this data
analyzer service account.

00:36:35.700 --> 00:36:38.850
And this as well you
can set permissions on.

00:36:42.514 --> 00:36:45.340
And you can see that data
scientists have the service

00:36:45.340 --> 00:36:47.500
account actor role just
on that service account,

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:49.990
not on any other
in that project.

00:36:49.990 --> 00:36:52.079
So this now allows them
to create instances

00:36:52.079 --> 00:36:54.620
that run as this service account
to query that BigQuery data.

00:36:58.201 --> 00:36:58.700
OK.

00:36:58.700 --> 00:37:04.055
Now, I'm going to login as
one of the data scientists.

00:37:04.055 --> 00:37:06.555
It's a very secretive company,
so her last name is redacted.

00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:10.020
All right.

00:37:10.020 --> 00:37:13.200
Now, we are looking at this from
Alice's point of view, who's

00:37:13.200 --> 00:37:16.610
a member of the data
scientist group.

00:37:16.610 --> 00:37:21.840
So she has access to the
data science dev project.

00:37:21.840 --> 00:37:25.680
She could create
instances there.

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:27.780
Let's see if she just tried
to snoop around a bit.

00:37:31.220 --> 00:37:33.530
So you can see
that in this case,

00:37:33.530 --> 00:37:35.510
Alice and no data
scientists has access

00:37:35.510 --> 00:37:39.969
to view the IAM policies
for this project,

00:37:39.969 --> 00:37:42.260
but they do have access to
some of the other resources,

00:37:42.260 --> 00:37:44.990
like Compute Engine.

00:37:44.990 --> 00:37:46.430
Let's go and create an instance.

00:37:50.020 --> 00:37:52.840
We'll put it here in US Central.

00:37:52.840 --> 00:37:57.760
So the first thing is, which
image are we going to use?

00:37:57.760 --> 00:38:00.680
There are the
Google-provided images.

00:38:00.680 --> 00:38:07.020
There's a variety of OSes there,
including Windows as well.

00:38:07.020 --> 00:38:09.770
We're going to go
over to Custom Images.

00:38:09.770 --> 00:38:11.370
And we can choose
to look at custom

00:38:11.370 --> 00:38:12.990
images in these projects.

00:38:12.990 --> 00:38:14.420
There's none in
data science dev.

00:38:14.420 --> 00:38:16.270
But if you look at
the images project,

00:38:16.270 --> 00:38:18.849
there we have our
custom Ubuntu V2 image.

00:38:18.849 --> 00:38:20.640
That's the latest one
that's been approved,

00:38:20.640 --> 00:38:21.580
so we'll use that one.

00:38:24.090 --> 00:38:27.400
Now we have our choice
of service accounts.

00:38:27.400 --> 00:38:30.000
We'll choose the Data
Analyzer service account.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:33.540
And note that when you choose
one other than the default,

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:36.510
we set the Cloud platform
scope for you automatically.

00:38:36.510 --> 00:38:39.540
Which means you just need
to think about the IAM roles

00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:41.880
and not about access
scopes to make sure

00:38:41.880 --> 00:38:44.490
that the instance and your
code has access to the APIs

00:38:44.490 --> 00:38:44.990
you need.

00:38:49.230 --> 00:38:52.790
And for the last piece, we
will use a shared network.

00:38:52.790 --> 00:38:55.120
You see we have
the XPN dev network

00:38:55.120 --> 00:38:57.650
that we've shared subnets from.

00:38:57.650 --> 00:39:00.620
And we can connect to that.

00:39:00.620 --> 00:39:02.660
So now we'll create
our instance using

00:39:02.660 --> 00:39:07.010
shared image, shared subnet,
and using only the service

00:39:07.010 --> 00:39:10.120
account that Alice and the other
data scientists have access to.

00:39:22.420 --> 00:39:22.920
All right.

00:39:22.920 --> 00:39:26.970
So now, let's SSH in there.

00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:30.000
And you'll see we're going
to transfer the SSH key.

00:39:34.610 --> 00:39:36.250
This says generating
a new key pair,

00:39:36.250 --> 00:39:38.260
sending the public
key into the instance,

00:39:38.260 --> 00:39:40.450
and then using the private
key to setup the session.

00:39:58.510 --> 00:40:00.480
All right.

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:02.160
Now, just to show
you how easy it

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:04.897
is if you're using the
client libraries to access

00:40:04.897 --> 00:40:06.480
the data that you
need in Google Cloud

00:40:06.480 --> 00:40:08.970
from within a
virtual machine, I'm

00:40:08.970 --> 00:40:13.380
going to install the client
library for BigQuery.

00:40:33.257 --> 00:40:33.757
OK.

00:40:41.730 --> 00:40:44.980
So here is the
quick Python script.

00:40:44.980 --> 00:40:48.960
We import the client library,
instantiate the client.

00:40:48.960 --> 00:40:50.680
And then we just
